276
00:29:31,200 --> 00:29:34,560
[SPEAKER_01] I like to think about the value to noise ratio.

277
00:29:35,040 --> 00:29:51,600
[SPEAKER_01] So what I mean by that is I think a lot of not just consumer products but tech products in general fall into this trap of just maximizing value and a fallacy that a lot of companies fall into is like well more products and more features equals more value and therefore we should just ship products and features as fast as we possibly can.

278
00:29:51,920 --> 00:30:09,840
[SPEAKER_01] But the reason that's the wrong way to think about it is the bottleneck is the capacity of a human brain to absorb the most valuable parts of your product experien gets more bloated and complex, sure absolute value of the product, if you just sum the number of products and features goes up.

279
00:30:10,320 --> 00:30:16,080
[SPEAKER_01] But the amount of noise and complexity also goes up, which means your value to noise ratio may drastically decline.

280
00:30:16,640 --> 00:30:36,480
[SPEAKER_01] And so what that means is as you're adding new products and features, you need to be constantly talking to users, analyzing user engagement rates, the correlation between feature usage and long term retention and subscriber retention rates to understand what your HERO features are and then pruning away.

281
00:30:36,960 --> 00:30:42,800
[SPEAKER_01] the products and features that are no longer adding any value so that your value to noise ratio stays as high as possible.

