352
00:39:15,360 --> 00:39:24,560
[SPEAKER_00] These new AI experiences are those like really fresh things that are more likely to catch attention and go viral and things like that.

353
00:39:24,720 --> 00:39:26,880
[SPEAKER_00] So, yeah, so many ways to do this these days.

354
00:39:28,400 --> 00:39:34,240
[SPEAKER_00] All right, tip number three, using AI tools to build and test hundreds of creatives per month.

355
00:39:34,800 --> 00:40:03,680
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, and so this is probably one of the more more standard tips that I imagine a lot of companies are already using, because it's pretty straightforward but it's very important, which is if you are relying on paid advertising as one of your channels to grow, and most subscription apps are, then there are tools you can use now, like Eleven Labs when it comes to voice, or like VO three when it comes to video, that will allow you to just exponentially increase the number of creatives you can test in any given month.

356
00:40:04,480 --> 00:40:07,200
[SPEAKER_01] And so an example that I use here is Runna.

357
00:40:07,520 --> 00:40:19,120
[SPEAKER_01] Runna went from, you know, tens of creative concepts per month to 400 plus creative concepts per month that their marketing team is spinning off each month.

358
00:40:19,920 --> 00:40:26,080
[SPEAKER_01] And when you think about the implications that has for the company's growth trajectory, they're massive.

359
00:40:26,240 --> 00:40:43,680
[SPEAKER_01] Not just because more creatives leads to more AV testing, leads to lower CACs, because you're figuring out which creative is working faster, and you're able to optimize and double down on your best performing agg creatives through networks like Meta and TikTok, but it also leads to more rapid learning cycle, right?

360
00:40:43,920 --> 00:40:50,800
[SPEAKER_01] You're learning more about your users and what resonates with them, which can inform product roadmap decisions, in addition to just paid user acquisition.

361
00:40:51,360 --> 00:40:58,400
[SPEAKER_01] So this is another example where the subscription value loop has these three discrete steps, value creation, value delivery and value capture, but they're really all connected as a system.

362
00:40:58,640 --> 00:41:04,320
[SPEAKER_01] And oftentimes the best teams are finding ways to make investments in one step that also positively impacts other steps in the loop.

363
00:41:04,800 --> 00:41:10,560
[SPEAKER_00] How do you think about the downsides of this flood of creative though?

364
00:41:11,040 --> 00:41:12,320
[SPEAKER_00] And I'll name one specific.

365
00:41:12,800 --> 00:41:23,280
[SPEAKER_00] The FTC had a ruling, I think, a year ago, and it applies more broadly than AI, but it's essentially that you can't fake user testimonials.

366
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:36,320
[SPEAKER_00] So technically, if you're creating an AI video or a slideshow or whatever, and it's like a person, even if it's a made up person, and that person is saying, you know, I used Runna to crush my five k.

367
00:41:36,880 --> 00:41:46,480
[SPEAKER_00] And if it's not a real person and it's not a real testimony in the US, that's technically against FTC guidelines and you can be fined and all that.

368
00:41:47,040 --> 00:41:52,400
[SPEAKER_00] How are you thinking about navigating the kind of truthfulness of it, the preserving the brand?

369
00:41:53,040 --> 00:42:00,640
[SPEAKER_00] I mean, yes, you can just spit out hundreds and hundreds of creatives, but how do you do that thoughtfully and carefully and in ways that won't?

370
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:02,960
[SPEAKER_00] get you fine in the long run.

371
00:42:03,360 --> 00:42:04,640
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, I'm so glad you brought this up.

372
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:19,920
[SPEAKER_01] And again, we could do a whole episode on this, but the short version is there's a difference between creating AI powered ads wholesale through a platform like VO three that's handling everything in to end, right?

373
00:42:20,240 --> 00:42:38,960
[SPEAKER_01] The video, the music, the person's voice versus using a real human being, but using tools like Eleven Labs for Voice or Suno for background music that allow you to just create a lot more permutations of the same ad unit at the same time.

374
00:42:39,440 --> 00:42:46,560
[SPEAKER_01] And so what I've advised my clients is to be very, very careful about going the wholesale AI route and using a tool like VO three.

375
00:42:46,960 --> 00:42:52,320
[SPEAKER_01] One, because these tools are still evolving, and I don't know if they're quite at the point yet where those ads are fully convincing.

376
00:42:52,480 --> 00:43:02,720
[SPEAKER_01] Two, because of the regulatory risk you brought up, and three, because there are a lot of consumers who have a really negative reaction if they detect that you're using an AI ad, which is increasingly hard to do, but it's a risk, right?

377
00:43:02,960 --> 00:43:05,920
[SPEAKER_01] Because if that blows back in your face, it could negatively impact your brand.

378
00:43:06,480 --> 00:43:12,960
[SPEAKER_01] But I think the risks are much lower, and the benefits are equally high if you can create real organic content.

379
00:43:13,600 --> 00:43:24,480
[SPEAKER_01] coming from real human beings, but that just layers on eleven labs technology so that you can take one spokesperson and translate their message into thirty different languages using AI.

380
00:43:24,960 --> 00:43:35,280
[SPEAKER_01] Or take one thirty second ad unit on YouTube or TikTok and then use Suno to layer in multiple different background music tracks and see which one performs best.

381
00:43:35,680 --> 00:43:38,320
[SPEAKER_01] For me, that's the right place to be playing in right now.

382
00:43:38,560 --> 00:44:03,120
[SPEAKER_00] And one of the better uses too for diversification of creatives is also just using it as a brainstorming partner is that, you know, mining your user reviews, mining feedback you get, mining, anything you can get your hands on competitors, competitor ads, and then using that to be able to more quickly iterate and try new things as well, not just like you said, wholesale creating assets using AI.

383
00:44:04,480 --> 00:44:10,400
[SPEAKER_00] All right, tip number four, highlighting AI features to reduce CAC and unlock new markets.

384
00:44:11,920 --> 00:44:17,680
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, so I mentioned Photo Room briefly on the last tip, but this is one where Photo Room has really shined.

385
00:44:18,080 --> 00:44:29,440
[SPEAKER_01] I had Olivier Lemarie on a podcast episode last year, and he talked about how historically, Photoroom had really struggled to penetrate some of these longer tail emerging markets.

386
00:44:29,680 --> 00:44:32,640
[SPEAKER_01] So countries like Mexico, Brazil, Indonesia.

387
00:44:33,200 --> 00:44:38,720
[SPEAKER_01] And the reason for that was Photoroom, like many consumer apps, is heavily dependent on paid user acquisition to scale.

388
00:44:39,920 --> 00:44:55,360
[SPEAKER_01] But they just didn't have sufficiently high conversion rates in those markets in order to get the unit economics to work through paid acquisition channels, even though the average cost for an impression on ad networks in these markets tends to be quite a bit lower than in markets like the US.

389
00:44:56,000 --> 00:45:21,280
[SPEAKER_01] But when they launched their new AI powered background feature where you could remove a background or replace a background feround or replaced a background using AI, not only did they see subscriber conversion rates in the product go up significantly, but they also found that by leading with those AI features in ad concepts on Meta and TikTok, they got much better performance, especially when they put it within the first six seconds of the ad.

390
00:45:21,680 --> 00:45:23,120
[SPEAKER_01] It just really caught users' attention.

391
00:45:23,280 --> 00:45:25,120
[SPEAKER_01] It goes back to the automagical experience, right?

392
00:45:25,280 --> 00:45:34,560
[SPEAKER_01] If I'm watching a ad with the tenth version of a calorie tracker or a language learning tool, it's like, okay, I've seen this before and I'm like next.

393
00:45:36,160 --> 00:45:44,480
[SPEAKER_01] I see this AI powered background removal feature as someone who's interested in photography that I've never seen before, then I'm much more likely to click through and give the product a chance.

394
00:45:45,120 --> 00:45:59,120
[SPEAKER_01] And so not only did this significantly improve their subscriber CAC numbers and their subscriber conversion rates, but it also fundamentally unlocked new international markets that they previously were unable to go into at all.

395
00:45:59,520 --> 00:46:07,760
[SPEAKER_00] We've already talked a lot about AI unlocking these kind of features that go viral and that are really powerful in user generated content and stuff like that.

396
00:46:07,920 --> 00:46:21,440
[SPEAKER_00] And so it makes a ton of sense that those same kind of principles apply to your paid ad spend as well., and getting that into the onboarding and getting it into the product in ways that really drive that excitement about the product and everything.

397
00:46:22,000 --> 00:46:23,600
[SPEAKER_00] So let's move on to value capture.

398
00:46:23,680 --> 00:46:25,760
[SPEAKER_00] And we've got four tips under value capture.

399
00:46:26,320 --> 00:46:30,960
[SPEAKER_00] The first is using cheaper, faster LLMs when performance is good enough.

400
00:46:31,600 --> 00:46:34,000
[SPEAKER_00] This is a, I'm going to push back on this one a little bit.

401
00:46:34,320 --> 00:46:35,520
[SPEAKER_00] Why do you think that's okay?

402
00:46:36,240 --> 00:46:38,240
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, and welcome a healthy debate on this one.

403
00:46:38,480 --> 00:46:48,640
[SPEAKER_01] And I will admit, it's controversial because at this point, you know, the best OpenAI and Anthropic models are just so powerful that it can be easy to just say, well, why would we try anything else?

404
00:46:49,280 --> 00:47:01,680
[SPEAKER_01] But Gamma is a great example of a company that when they were first getting started, they famously had this viral tweet in March 2023 when they launched AI powered presentations that fundamentally altered the trajectory of their business.

405
00:47:02,160 --> 00:47:03,760
[SPEAKER_01] And six months later, they were profitable.

406
00:47:04,320 --> 00:47:14,960
[SPEAKER_01] And there are a number of reasons why they got to profitability so quickly, but one of them was they were very savvy in the underlying LLMs they used to power the early versions of their AI powered presentations product.

407
00:47:15,520 --> 00:47:51,200
[SPEAKER_01] Which is not to say they weren't ever using the most powerful models, but in some cases what they found was using some of these longer tail models, not only was the performance good enough in terms of the quality of the output that they were delivering to their users, but also the speed of the product was significantly faster, and the cost to the business of the compute power needed to serve these AI powered features was significantly lower, which as a startup that was fighting for its survival in early 2023 was really important.

408
00:47:52,000 --> 00:48:00,880
[SPEAKER_01] And so it's just a lesson that at this point we're all like waiting with bated breath for the next release of the hot new OpenAI Red Thrones.

409
00:48:00,880 --> 00:48:01,520
[SPEAKER_01] open AI or entropic model.

410
00:48:01,680 --> 00:48:02,080
[SPEAKER_01] And that's great.

411
00:48:02,240 --> 00:48:06,320
[SPEAKER_01] Like the frontier of what these models can do is so fascinating and compelling.

412
00:48:07,440 --> 00:48:11,840
[SPEAKER_01] But don't forget that there's this long tail of thousands and thousands of other models out there.

413
00:48:12,080 --> 00:48:16,480
[SPEAKER_01] And sometimes those models tailored to your use case can be the optimal solution.

414
00:48:16,800 --> 00:48:30,000
[SPEAKER_00] I wanted to push back when I saw this one, as we were prepping for the podcast, because I think where you draw the line of what is a good experience or not is where this can be a trap.

415
00:48:30,320 --> 00:48:32,720
[SPEAKER_00] And we've been talking about this a lot inside RevenueCAD.

416
00:48:32,720 --> 00:48:43,920
[SPEAKER_00] We just released this really cool chatbot where you can talk to your data at RevenueCat and you can ask it things that you would otherwise, you know, spend five minutes looking up in the dashboard with all these filters and stuff.

417
00:48:44,160 --> 00:48:50,240
[SPEAKER_00] You can ask it things you couldn't even find in the dashboard because it can combine things in ways that the dashboard doesn't currently enable.

418
00:48:50,880 --> 00:48:59,440
[SPEAKER_00] And we are using the highest end models and we're doing that because we want the product experience to be the absolute best it can possibly be.

419
00:49:00,240 --> 00:49:04,320
[SPEAKER_00] And we're doing that with the recognition that over time that should get cheaper.

420
00:49:04,720 --> 00:49:11,200
[SPEAKER_00] So I think you need to be careful not to over optimize too soon and figure out exactly when it makes sense.

421
00:49:11,680 --> 00:49:12,880
[SPEAKER_00] I like the way you put it though.

422
00:49:13,440 --> 00:49:20,000
[SPEAKER_00] Sometimes you can create a better experience with a less powerful model if that model is tailored to that specific use case.

423
00:49:20,640 --> 00:49:39,520
[SPEAKER_00] For example, I've been tinkering with a little vibe coded app that I've been working on, and I actually think Gemini and Gemini Flash specifically is probably the best experience for that app, because Gemini is really great for some of some things that OpenAI and Anthropic models aren't actually good at, and it just also happens to be cheaper.

424
00:49:39,920 --> 00:50:04,160
[SPEAKER_00] But I think if you're trying to create the best possible experience and you're selling it at a premium price and you're trying to get people excited about it, you just have to be reason really careful not kind of prematurely optimizing on cost versus just going for that best possible experience, especially in this age where the competition is so fast where you're going to have a competitor come out and copy that feature but maybe use the better model.

425
00:50:04,960 --> 00:50:07,680
[SPEAKER_00] Any follow-up thoughts to push back or echo what I said?

426
00:50:08,160 --> 00:50:15,200
[SPEAKER_01] I think it's such a great point and I'm glad you're pushing on this, but I would argue the two points don't have to contradict one another.

427
00:50:15,600 --> 00:50:30,480
[SPEAKER_01] So what I'm arguing is, one, the product experience is a function not just of the output that the LLM provides, but also how fast it provides that output and how expensive it is to generate that output.

428
00:50:30,960 --> 00:50:43,280
[SPEAKER_01] And so if you are on the bleeding edge of a prosumer category where peak performance is the thing that matters and cost is no object, then absolutely go use the most powerful open AI or anthropic model.

429
00:50:43,760 --> 00:51:00,560
[SPEAKER_01] But to use your analogy before, if you're CalAI competing with my fitness pal and your target market is a 20 something young professional who is not yet at the point where they can afford the most premium tool on the market and a faster, cheaper model is good enough, then it may very well.

430
00:51:01,120 --> 00:51:05,600
[SPEAKER_01] may be that you're not best served using OpenAI or Anthropic's latest model.

431
00:51:06,080 --> 00:51:15,520
[SPEAKER_01] The point you're making, which I think is equally valid, but I think you can reconcile it with the first point, is all of these models are seeing their costs decline pretty rapidly.

432
00:51:15,760 --> 00:51:19,360
[SPEAKER_01] And over time, I think there's going to be more and more commoditization of the models, right?

433
00:51:19,600 --> 00:51:32,320
[SPEAKER_01] Like Deepseek was big whatever it was a year or two ago, because they came out of nowhere in China, and their performance was every bit as good at one point as some of the top OpenAI or Anthropic models, and that has changed.

434
00:51:32,720 --> 00:51:39,040
[SPEAKER_01] All that competition is creating downward pressure on the pricing of what these models charge for their API use.

435
00:51:39,840 --> 00:51:45,040
[SPEAKER_01] What that means is you should be thinking about where things trend over the next six, twelve to twelve months, not just the short term.

436
00:51:45,920 --> 00:51:46,800
[SPEAKER_01] I think both can be true.

437
00:51:47,200 --> 00:52:01,280
[SPEAKER_01] Consider who your target customer is and how much speed and affordability matters relative to pure performance as you decide which LLM to use, but then also keep in mind that the cost of these LLMs will come down quickly over the next six to twelve months and factor that into your calculations.

438
00:52:02,160 --> 00:52:07,680
[SPEAKER_00] Let's move on to tip number two, offering basic versus AI powered subscription tiers.

439
00:52:08,720 --> 00:52:18,480
[SPEAKER_01] This one is kind of obvious once you break it down, but I think the key point is that it used to be the marginal cost of serving a user or subscriber for most consumer or pro-summer subscription apps was close to zero.

440
00:52:18,880 --> 00:52:33,520
[SPEAKER_01] Unless you were a hardware product like Oura or Whoop or you were say a meal kit or physical goods service like Blue Apron or Stitch Fix, your underlying cost structure was very minimal because you're just a software business.

441
00:52:34,640 --> 00:52:41,040
[SPEAKER_01] But now with these AI powered products and features, that's no longer true because you do have the underlying compute costs to support the AI features.

442
00:52:41,520 --> 00:52:47,360
[SPEAKER_01] We just talked about how the cost of LLMs is coming down pretty rapidly, but it's still a non trivial cost structure for these companies to support.

